 "text": "Your next deadline won't kill you, but the first ones could. 1860s, the American Civil War. In prison camps like Andersonville, Georgia, guards marked a line near the walls, usually a low fence. Cross it, and you could be shot. They called it the deadline. Later, printers and newspapers borrowed the word, until it meant a time limit. Now it just means, get it done. Every legend has a beginning.",
